Bias in, Bias out: Annotation Bias in Multilingual Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14662v1)
citeturn6526view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({"ref_id":"turn6523view1","pattern":"ensemble"}); Total lines: 401
L200: Weighted ensemble methods can address this by leveraging multiple model perspectives to smooth over annotation noise, while still preserving minority viewpoints.
L201: #### Post-hoc Model Adjustment
L202: 
L203: Biases in trained models can sometimes be mitigated using post-hoc correction methods such as embedding debiasing or output regularisation. cite98†Kaneko et al. (2023) proposed modifying model outputs by subtracting a learned bias component:
L204: 
L205:  | $$f^{\text{debias}}(x)=f_{\theta}(x)-\lambda b(x)$$  |  | (12)
L206: 
L207: where $f_{\theta}(x)$ is the original model output, $b(x)$ is a bias projection and $\lambda$ controls debiasing strength.
L208: #### Fine-tuning and In-context Debiasing
L209: 
L210: Recent work has explored using targeted fine-tuning (cite99†Webster et al., 2021 ) or in-context prompting cite100†Ganguli et al. (2023) to reshape model behaviour without altering the training data (cite101†Kaneko et al., 2025 ). In the fine-tuning case, model parameters $\theta$ are updated using reweighted or re-annotated dataset $\mathcal{D}^{*}$:
L211: 
L212:  | $$\min_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{*})\sim\mathcal{D}^{*}}\mathcal{L}(f_{\theta}(x),y^{*})$$  |  | (13)
L213: In in-context learning, models are conditioned on carefully constructed prompts $P$ that reduce bias:
L214: 
L215:  | $$f_{\theta}(y\mid x,P)$$  |  | (14)
L216: 
L217: where $P$ is designed to reduce the likelihood of biased completions while preserving task accuracy.
L218: #### Multi-Objective Weighted Ensemble Learning
L219: Another reactive strategy leverages ensemble learning to mitigate annotation bias by explicitly modelling annotator disagreement (cite93†Geva et al., 2019 ). Given a dataset $\mathcal{D}=\{(x_{i},\{y_{i}^{(j)}\}_{j=1}^{M})\}_{i=1}^{N}$, cite102†Huang et al. (2025) proposed Weak Ensemble Learning (WEL), which samples one annotator label per instance to construct $K$ label-variant datasets.
L220: Each trains a weak predictor $f_{\theta_{k}}$, weighted by its held-out performance (e.g., F1, cross-entropy, Manhattan distance), with $\sum_{k=1}^{K}w_{k}=1$. We extend WEL to a multilingual setting by applying the same label-sampling procedure across datasets in different languages using a shared multilingual model. Final predictions are computed as:
L221:  | $\displaystyle\hat{y}_{i}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}w_{k}\,f_{\theta_{k}}(x_{i}),$  |  | (15)
L222: 
L223: allowing the ensemble to capture annotator disagreement while leveraging multilingual representations from a single model.
L224: We use mBERT (cite40†Devlin et al., 2019 ) as the base model. On the multi-source benchmark from the LeWiDi 2023 shared task (cite103†Leonardellli et al., 2023 ), WEL generally outperforms baselines using single-model CE loss (CE-only) (cite104†Uma et al., 2020 ) and majority-vote ensembles of top five annotators (Top-5-Ann) (cite105†Xu et al., 2024 ), achieving higher F1 and lower CE/MD scores.
L225: The only exception is ArMIS, where the very small annotator pool (three annotators) limits the effectiveness of random label sampling. As the primary focus of this paper is on the discussion of annotation bias in multilingual LLMs, we include the full experimental results in Appendix cite35†B .
