Tell Me: An LLM-powered Mental Well-being Assistant with RAG, Synthetic Dialogue Generation, and Agentic Planning (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14445v1)
citeturn6526view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({"ref_id":"turn6523view3","pattern":"Safety"}); Total lines: 232
L85: These advances highlight potential, but also underscore persistent concerns about reliability, safety, and confidentiality. Access to authentic therapeutic dialogues is restricted, motivating profile-conditioned synthetic dialogues as a safer alternative cite32†Wang et al. (2024) .
L86: Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) improves factuality and relevance in dialogue cite45†Lewis et al. (2020) ; cite46†Shuster et al. (2021) , with more recent systems such as BlenderBot 3 cite47†Shuster et al. (2022) , INFO-RAG cite48†Xu et al. (2024) , and R2AG cite49†Ye et al. (2024) extending retrieval for open-domain conversation, safety, and long-term grounding.
L87: Our system differs by applying RAG specifically to mental well-being, where the goal is not only factuality, but also reflective, empathetic, and context-sensitive dialogue. This positions Tell Me as a demonstration of how retrieval grounding can enhance responsible interaction in emotionally sensitive domains.
L88: Parallel efforts in healthcare explore agentic AI, including multiagent dialogue support for clinicians cite50†Kampman et al. (2024) and psychiatric interview assistants cite51†Bi et al. (2025) . These works primarily target diagnostic or clinician-facing use cases. In contrast, our system employs CrewAI-based orchestration to extend end-user support to dynamic planning and guided meditation, addressing the limitations of static wellness tools.
L89: Tell Me is the first open demo to showcase three independent but complementary modules: a context-aware RAG assistant, a synthetic dialogue generator addressing data scarcity, and an agentic planner for adaptive self-care. Presented side by side, they highlight different pathways for advancing safe and customizable well-being support.
L92: Tell Me comprises three independent modules: (i) a RAG-based assistant for context-aware reflective dialogue, (ii) a synthetic conversation generator for enhancing research data, and (iii) a CrewAI-based planner for end-user self-care. Although these components are not tightly integrated, they are intentionally presented together to illustrate complementary applications of LLM in mental well-being.
L93: End-users can engage with the assistant and planner for reflective support, while researchers can generate safe synthetic transcripts for experimentation. All three modules are accessible through a unified demo interface implemented in Streamlit (cite53†Streamlit Inc., 2022 ), which provides a simple front-end to select functionality and interact with the system.
L94: ### 3.1 Tell Me RAG-based Assistant
L95: The RAG-based assistant forms the core client-facing module, providing context-aware responses grounded in a curated knowledge base. Unlike baseline LLMs that often provide generic or verbose advice, this assistant integrates embedding, retrieval, and context management to maintain conversational history while offering relevant and grounded responses.
L96: A sentiment-based safety prefilter intercepts high-risk content, stopping free-form generation, and issuing predefined safety messages when necessary (Figure cite54†1 ). This enables Tell Me to simulate therapist-style interactions in a controlled, responsible manner.
L97: #### 3.1.1 Data and Knowledge Base
L98: 
L99: To address confidentiality constraints, we adopt a Context–Response schema where context represents a client’s input and response represents a therapist-like reply. For our prototype, we use two open-source datasets—Counsel Chat (cite55†DeMasi et al., 2019 ) and Mental Health Counseling Conversations (cite56†Amod, 2023 )—reformatted into this schema to serve as the retrieval base.
L100: #### 3.1.2 Embedding, Retrieval, and Context Management
L101: We embed the dataset using BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5 (cite57†Beijing Academy of Artificial Intelligence, 2023 ), storing vectors in a LlamaIndex index for efficient semantic search. The retriever identifies the top-3 relevant responses, re-ranked by a custom query engine tuned to therapeutic tone. Multi-turn interaction is supported via a Context Chat Engine that maintains a memory buffer of prior turns, allowing conversations to remain coherent and contextually grounded.
L102: In the demo, users type a concern into a chatbox and receive context-aware responses in real time.
L128: We evaluate nine representative models spanning frontier LLMs and well-being–tuned systems (cite69†OpenAI, 2024 ; cite70†Touvron et al., 2023 ; cite71†Jiang et al., 2023 ; cite72†Gemma Team, 2024 ; cite73†Anthropic, 2025 ; cite74†Microsoft Research, 2024 ): GPT-4o, LLaMA-3, Mistral-7B, Gemma-3, Claude 3.7 Sonnet, and Phi-4 Mini 3.8B, plus three Ollama-based models.^{5}^{5} 5 vitorcalvi/mentallama2:latest, wmb/llamasupport, ALIENTELLIGENCE/mentalwellness. Each model answers all prompts. The top performer advances to RAG vs.
L129: non-RAG ablations (Section cite22†4.4 ).
L130: ### 4.2 LLM-as-a-Judge
L131: We use GPT-5 as the primary judge (deterministic decoding) and GPT-4o as a secondary judge for consistency.^{6}^{6} 6 Decoding parameters are fixed; reasoning models that ignore temperature use defaults. Each answer is scored 1–10 across five dimensions: Safety, Empathy, Usefulness, Clarity, and Overall quality (weights: 3/3/2/2/2). Scores and short justifications are returned for every Prompt$\times$Model pair; averages determine rankings, and the top model is advanced.
L132: In total, the judges produced 180 scored outputs (9 models $\times$ 10 prompts $\times$ 2 judges). We also collect per-prompt notes highlighting tone, empathy, and safety issues.
L133: ### 4.3 Prompt-Level Comparative Analysis
L134: 
L135: To complement scalar scores, we run a post-hoc comparative chain where the judge summarizes strengths (e.g., empathetic framing) and issues (e.g., diagnostic overreach, safety lapses) across ordered model answers. This analysis never re-scores or alters rankings but provides qualitative insight into where and why models differ.
L136: ### 4.4 Human Evaluation (Phase II)
L137: We conducted a within-subject evaluation comparing the RAG and non-RAG setups. We recruited 10 adult participants (ages 25–35; 5 female, 5 male), all working professionals, of whom 4 had previously used AI systems (e.g., ChatGPT) to reflect on emotions. Each participant evaluated five randomized prompt pairs (RAG vs. non-RAG, order-blinded), yielding 50 judgments in total. Ratings were collected on a 5-point scale across five dimensions: Helpfulness, Supportiveness, Clarity, Groundedness, and Overall.
L138: All sessions included disclaimers and crisis resources; participation was voluntary and anonymous.^{7}^{7} 7 Survey items and anonymized transcripts are released in the GitHub repository: cite37†https://github.com/trystine/Tell_Me_Mental_Wellbeing_System†github.com L139: ### 4.5 Discussion and Limitations
L140: The evaluation shows initial evidence that retrieval grounding improves contextuality and empathy in well-being dialogue. However, several limitations remain. The LLM-as-a-judge approach may reflect biases of the judge models, the human study relied on a small pool of nonclinical participants, and only the assistant module was evaluated; the synthetic dialogue generator and planner were not subject to formal testing. We leave larger-scale, expert-involved studies for future work.
L162: In general, these findings underscore that effective mental health assistants must prioritize safety, empathy, and invitational dialogue. Models that foster user agency (Claude, Gemma-3, LLaMA-3) performed best, while those that pathologize, minimize or over-assist scored lowest.
L171: Future work will expand evaluation beyond the assistant. For the Simulate a Conversation module, we aim to benchmark dialogue quality, empathy, and safety across different model families, moving beyond the current reliance on GPT-4o. We also plan to explore specialized evaluations aligned with therapeutic domains (e.g., CBT, trauma, depression) and diversify cultural and demographic profiles to generate more inclusive synthetic dialogues.
L176: To mitigate risks, we restrict outputs to reflective dialogue, include disclaimers clarifying non-clinical use, and incorporate safeguards such as a safety prefilter. Synthetic dialogues reduce reliance on confidential clinical data, but cannot fully capture the nuance of real therapeutic exchanges. Similarly, the planner generates personalized suggestions and meditations, but these routines are not medically validated.
