L205: 4) Evaluation Metrics: Two quantitative metrics are used to evaluate the proposed method: Manipulation Success Rate (MSR), the proportion of robot actions, after steering, that successfully perform downstream manipulation tasks; and Steering Objective Alignment (SOA), the proportion of selected action proposals that align with the intended steering objective. For each method and task scenario, we perform 20 trials and report the mean performance.
L206: ### IV-B Results
L207: 1) Can VLA-Pilot improve the pre-trained VLA policies across different downstream tasks? To answer this question, we integrate VLA-Pilot with two pre-trained VLA policies, namely DiVLA and RDT-1B. As shown in Table cite57†I , VLA-Pilot consistently improves the performance of both pre-trained VLA policies in all six downstream tasks, achieving average improvements of +0.31 MSR for DiVLA and +0.30 MSR for RDT-1B.
L208: Qualitative results in Figure cite55†5 further support these findings, demonstrating that the integration of VLA-Pilot facilitates zero-shot deployment of pre-trained VLA policies to downstream tasks. In contrast, off-the-shelf pre-trained VLA policies exhibit limited performance across most tasks.
L209: We find that most failure cases of these pre-trained policies stem not from an inability to generate feasible actions, but from inconsistencies in selecting task-aligned actions due to observation discrepancies and action distributional shifts. For example, in the Mug Handling task, which involves diverse action modes such as grasping, lifting, and pouring, the pre-trained DiVLA policy attains an average MSR of 0.54, indicating that its generated action candidates include viable options.
L210: However, due to its inability to consistently select actions aligned with the task semantics, its final MSR is significantly lower than the version augmented with VLA-Pilot, which achieves a much higher MSR of 0.75.
L211: cite58†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 6: Visualization of left-arm action proposals in bimanual zippering tasks. (a) Baseline steering methods relying on post-ranking and selection fail due to lack of viable initial proposals; (b) increasing the number of proposals does not guarantee feasible actions; (c) our method employs Evolutionary Diffusion to evolve action proposals toward a target distribution with higher task alignment that is comparable to (d) fine-tuned policies.
L212: 2) How does VLA-Pilot compare to state-of-the-art policy steering methods? We compare VLA-Pilot with V-GPS and FOREWARN by integrating different steering methods with DiVLA. In the ID setting, VLA-Pilot outperforms both baselines in terms of average MSR and SOA (see Table cite57†I ). Notably, the performance gap between our method and the two baselines is minimal on simple tasks (e.g., Mug Handling, Bag Handling).
